WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.007
Hi, and welcome.

00:00:01.007 --> 00:00:04.845
A school club tries a speech-to-text tool for meeting notes.

00:00:04.845 --> 00:00:13.340
It works well for one speaker in a quiet room, then struggles when several students speak with different accents and background noise.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:23.157
As you watch, keep one question in mind: Fairness takes listening, checking different experiences, and giving people a practical way to challenge a result.

00:00:23.157 --> 00:00:25.989
You do not need to memorize a list of terms.

00:00:25.989 --> 00:00:33.288
Try to notice what the people in the story know, what they still need to check, and what choice they can make next.

00:00:33.838 --> 00:00:38.441
Imagine a team captain who only listens to the loudest people in a meeting.

00:00:38.441 --> 00:00:44.088
The plan might look successful on paper while some members barely get a chance to be heard.

00:00:44.088 --> 00:00:47.525
This comparison is a way to think, not a perfect match.

00:00:47.525 --> 00:00:54.215
Real AI systems can work in different ways, so it helps to ask what this particular tool was designed to do.

00:00:54.215 --> 00:00:57.713
Compare group results using rates instead of raw counts.

00:00:57.713 --> 00:01:01.150
What detail in the story makes that goal easier to see?

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:06.268
The club adds a quieter microphone and allows students to correct the notes.

00:01:06.268 --> 00:01:12.099
One change helps, but students still need another way to participate if the transcript is wrong.

00:01:12.099 --> 00:01:13.301
Pause for a moment.

00:01:13.301 --> 00:01:16.728
What changed between the first situation and the second?

00:01:16.728 --> 00:01:20.514
If the result changed too, that is a clue worth investigating.

00:01:20.514 --> 00:01:22.558
It is not proof of the cause yet.

00:01:22.558 --> 00:01:27.908
Think of one more example that could help separate a genuine pattern from a coincidence.

00:01:28.458 --> 00:01:33.560
A tool's overall score can hide whose words were understood and whose were missed.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:39.035
Looking at separate experiences can reveal a problem that an average keeps out of view.

00:01:39.035 --> 00:01:42.394
A useful explanation connects a detail to a decision.

00:01:42.394 --> 00:01:48.305
Try finishing this sentence in your own words: I would pay attention to this detail because...

00:01:48.305 --> 00:01:51.291
Identify representation and accessibility gaps.

00:01:51.291 --> 00:01:55.024
You can use a different example from the one in the lesson.

00:01:55.024 --> 00:01:59.130
The goal is to explain the reasoning, not to repeat a definition.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:05.683
Ask who is missing from the test, invite feedback from different students, and compare the errors.

00:02:05.683 --> 00:02:10.707
Decide what the school will do when the system gets someone's contribution wrong.

00:02:10.707 --> 00:02:14.934
Before looking at any result, say what you expect to happen and why.

00:02:14.934 --> 00:02:18.426
Then compare your prediction with what actually happens.

00:02:18.426 --> 00:02:24.184
If they do not match, keep the surprising result; it may be the most useful part of the test.

00:02:24.734 --> 00:02:29.228
If a new microphone improves the average, what else would you want to know?

00:02:29.228 --> 00:02:35.041
Consider whether every student can access it and whether corrections actually change the record.

00:02:35.041 --> 00:02:38.816
It is tempting to judge a tool by its most impressive example.

00:02:38.816 --> 00:02:45.407
A more careful check also asks who was not represented, what information is missing, and what could go wrong.

00:02:45.407 --> 00:02:47.984
Propose a safeguard and an appeal process.

00:02:47.984 --> 00:02:52.358
A person should be able to question a result and choose what to do next.

00:02:52.908 --> 00:02:55.921
Suppose two groups each contribute twenty clips.

00:02:55.921 --> 00:03:00.001
The tool gets nineteen from one group and twelve from the other.

00:03:00.001 --> 00:03:04.898
Before deployment, investigate why the experiences differ and who can appeal.

00:03:04.898 --> 00:03:06.906
Work through it in three moves.

00:03:06.906 --> 00:03:09.731
First, say what job the tool is meant to do.

00:03:09.731 --> 00:03:15.067
Next, point to the evidence you have, rather than a guess about how the tool thinks.

00:03:15.067 --> 00:03:18.080
Finally, decide what still needs a human check.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:22.788
There may be more than one reasonable answer if you explain your evidence.

00:03:23.338 --> 00:03:28.623
Now open the practice activity, Audit a fictional speech tool, when you are ready.

00:03:28.623 --> 00:03:32.747
In the fairness activity, follow the people behind each number.

00:03:32.747 --> 00:03:38.096
Look for both the measured result and the real opportunity someone gains or loses.

00:03:38.096 --> 00:03:40.739
Take a first try before asking for help.

00:03:40.739 --> 00:03:44.863
If feedback changes your mind, name the detail that changed it.

00:03:44.863 --> 00:03:48.730
If you keep your answer, explain what evidence supports it.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.541
Your lesson project is called Write a student-first deployment decision.

00:03:53.541 --> 00:03:58.454
For your project, name the students who may have the hardest time using your idea.

00:03:58.454 --> 00:04:03.603
Describe a safeguard those students can use, not just a promise that the tool is fair.

00:04:03.603 --> 00:04:08.101
Before you finish, imagine someone else using your idea for the first time.

00:04:08.101 --> 00:04:09.877
What would they need to know?

00:04:09.877 --> 00:04:13.073
What could they do if the tool did not work for them?

00:04:13.073 --> 00:04:16.328
Those questions can make a promising idea more useful.

00:04:16.878 --> 00:04:21.961
Pick one word from today's lesson and explain it using the story you just heard.

00:04:21.961 --> 00:04:24.693
Avoid copying the wording from the screen.

00:04:24.693 --> 00:04:28.188
You might begin, In this situation, this word means...

00:04:28.188 --> 00:04:32.000
If your explanation is hard to follow, add a small example.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:37.782
Clear language is a good way to notice what you understand and what you want to ask about.

00:04:38.332 --> 00:04:46.826
Here is one idea to take with you: Fairness takes listening, checking different experiences, and giving people a practical way to challenge a result.

00:04:46.826 --> 00:04:51.558
Think of a different class or school situation where the same question could help.

00:04:51.558 --> 00:04:55.833
You can write down one thing you would check and one person you could ask.

00:04:55.833 --> 00:05:00.508
That is enough for a useful next step; you do not have to solve every part today.

00:05:01.058 --> 00:05:03.478
Thanks for thinking this through with me.

00:05:03.478 --> 00:05:09.382
Finish the activity and project on the lesson page, then try the separate quiz when you feel ready.

00:05:09.382 --> 00:05:15.226
If a question feels difficult, return to the example and explain your reasoning in your own words.

00:05:15.226 --> 00:05:22.015
Fairness takes listening, checking different experiences, and giving people a practical way to challenge a result.

00:05:22.015 --> 00:05:24.317
The goal is not to trust every answer.

00:05:24.317 --> 00:05:28.154
It is to know what to check and how to make a thoughtful choice.
